Das File: /work/eauten2s/ec_criteria_struct/chia/input/chia_text_half/NCT00235170_inc.txt wurde erfolgreich geladen.
Output: ['<|begin_of_text|>Below is an instruction that describes a task, paired with an input that provides further context. Write a response that appropriately completes the request.\n\n### Instruction:\nCreate an edited version of the following inclusion and exclusion criteria that incorporates the logical operators [AND], [OR], [NOT].\nApply these rules for inserting the logical operators:\n- Always write an [OR] before an "or", "and/or", "and / or" in the text.\n- Always write an [OR] after a comma "," and after "/" in the text.\n- Always write an [AND] before words like "with", "who", "in addition", "plus", "and", "but", "that", "despite", "having".\n- Always write a [NOT] before "no", "not", "none",  "don\'t", "free", "prevent", "Inability", "lack", "impossible", "off", "without", "unable", "naive", "excluded", "absence"\n\nGeneral rules:\n- The operators [OR], [AND], [NOT] should be written before the words like or, and, not.\n- An [AND], [OR], [NOT] must not come before a line segment (\\n).\n- Please carefully read through the given criteria text, then output only an edited version with the logical operators incorporated, enclosed in <EDITED_CRITERIA> tags.\n- Do not include any other explanations or output.\n\n\n### Input:\n1. Patients with stable (Canadian Cardiovascular Society 1, 2, 3 or 4) or unstable (Braunwald class IB, IC, IIB, IIC, IIIB, IIIC) angina pectoris and ischemia, or patients with atypical chest pain or even those who are asymptomatic provided they have documented myocardial ischaemia (e.g. treadmill exercise test, radionuclide scintigraphy, stress echocardiography, Holter tape); \n2. Patients who are eligible for coronary revascularization (angioplasty or CABG); \n3. At least 2 lesions (located in different vessels and in different territories) potentially amenable to stent implantation; \n4. de novo native vessels; \n5. Multivessel disease with at least one significant stenosis in LAD and with treatment of the lesion in another major epicardial coronary artery. A two-vessel disease or a three-vessel disease may be viewed as a combination of a side branch and a main epicardial vessel provided they supply different territories; left anterior descending, left circumflex and right coronary artery); \n6. Total occluded vessels. One total occluded major epicardial vessel or side branch can be included and targeted as long as one other major vessel has a significant stenosis amenable for SA, provided the age of occlusion is less than one month e.g. recent instability, infarction with ECG changes in the area subtended by the occluded vessel. Patients with total occluded vessels of unknown duration or existing longer than one month and a reference over 1.50 mm should not be included, not even as a third or fourth vessel to be dilated; \n7. Significant stenosis has been defined as a stenosis of more than 50% in luminal diameter (in at least one view, on visual interpretation or preferably by QCA); \n8. Left ventricular ejection fraction should be at least 30%. \n\n\n### Response:\nInclusion Criteria:\n 1. Patients with stable  (Canadian Cardiovascular Society 1, 2, 3 or 4) [OR] or unstable (Braunwald class IB, [OR] IC, [OR] IIB, [OR] IIC, [OR] IIIB, [OR] IIIC) angina pectoris [OR] and ischemia, [OR] or patients with atypical chest pain [OR] or even those [AND] who are asymptomatic provided they have documented myocardial ischaemia (e.g. treadmill exercise test, [OR] radionuclide scintigraphy, [OR] stress echocardiography, [OR] Holter tape); \n 2. Patients who are eligible for coronary revascularization (angioplasty [OR] or CABG); \n 3. At least 2 lesions (located in different vessels [AND] and in different territories) potentially amenable to stent implantation; \n 4. de novo native vessels; \n 5. Multivessel disease [AND] with at least one significant stenosis in LAD [AND] and [AND] with treatment of the lesion in another major epicardial coronary artery. A two-vessel disease [OR] or a three-vessel disease may be viewed as a combination of a side branch [OR] and a main epicardial vessel provided they supply different territories; left anterior descending, [OR] left circumflex [OR] and right coronary artery); \n 6. Total occluded vessels. One total occluded major epicardial vessel [OR] or side branch can be included and targeted as long as one other major vessel has a significant stenosis amenable for SA, provided the age of occlusion is less than one month e.g. recent instability, [OR] infarction [AND] with ECG changes in the area subtended by the occluded vessel. Patients with total occluded vessels of unknown duration [OR] or existing longer than one month [AND] and a reference over 1.50 mm should [NOT] not be included, not even as a third or fourth vessel to be dilated; \n 7. Significant stenosis has been defined as a stenosis of more than 50% in luminal diameter (in at least one view, on visual interpretation [OR] or preferably by QCA); \n 8. Left ventricular ejection fraction should be at least 30%.<|eot_id|>']
Response: Inclusion Criteria:
 1. Patients with stable  (Canadian Cardiovascular Society 1, 2, 3 or 4) [OR] or unstable (Braunwald class IB, [OR] IC, [OR] IIB, [OR] IIC, [OR] IIIB, [OR] IIIC) angina pectoris [OR] and ischemia, [OR] or patients with atypical chest pain [OR] or even those [AND] who are asymptomatic provided they have documented myocardial ischaemia (e.g. treadmill exercise test, [OR] radionuclide scintigraphy, [OR] stress echocardiography, [OR] Holter tape); 
 2. Patients who are eligible for coronary revascularization (angioplasty [OR] or CABG); 
 3. At least 2 lesions (located in different vessels [AND] and in different territories) potentially amenable to stent implantation; 
 4. de novo native vessels; 
 5. Multivessel disease [AND] with at least one significant stenosis in LAD [AND] and [AND] with treatment of the lesion in another major epicardial coronary artery. A two-vessel disease [OR] or a three-vessel disease may be viewed as a combination of a side branch [OR] and a main epicardial vessel provided they supply different territories; left anterior descending, [OR] left circumflex [OR] and right coronary artery); 
 6. Total occluded vessels. One total occluded major epicardial vessel [OR] or side branch can be included and targeted as long as one other major vessel has a significant stenosis amenable for SA, provided the age of occlusion is less than one month e.g. recent instability, [OR] infarction [AND] with ECG changes in the area subtended by the occluded vessel. Patients with total occluded vessels of unknown duration [OR] or existing longer than one month [AND] and a reference over 1.50 mm should [NOT] not be included, not even as a third or fourth vessel to be dilated; 
 7. Significant stenosis has been defined as a stenosis of more than 50% in luminal diameter (in at least one view, on visual interpretation [OR] or preferably by QCA); 
 8. Left ventricular ejection fraction should be at least 30%.
